District-heating dispatch models are widely simplified, yet the resulting decision cost is rarely quantified.
We distinguish estimation bias—the objective difference between formulations—from decision regret—the execution cost of each dispatch
schedule when re-simulated using a common high-fidelity forward model with exponential heat losses, computed-friction hydraulics,
and consumer differential-pressure constraints. We evaluate these metrics using a one-phenomenon-per-step fidelity ladder, from a copperplate
model to station-resolved hydraulics. A copperplate model supplied with aggregate losses enables an exact decomposition of loss and topology effects.
Tests cover the real Memmingen network and a balanced factorial of 135 synthetic networks. The copperplate underestimates operating cost by
15.1\% but produces schedules costing 46.1\% more to execute, whereas loss-aware schedules are physically deliverable. Loss visibility, rather
than spatial topology, explains this discrepancy: 95.8\% of the copperplate-to-baseline gap is attributable to losses and 4.7\% to topology.
Across the synthetic networks, losses explain a median 100.0\% of the gap; the topology effect remains within $\pm 0.6$\% for trunk lengths of at
least 5\,km and never exceeds 2.4\%. Aggregate loss adders can conceal the deficiency for one network but do not transfer across scenarios.
Resolving all 174 transmission stations and service laterals using real component data changes dispatch cost by less than 1\% and decisions negligibly,
locating the useful fidelity threshold below station-level hydraulics. Spatial routing is likewise immaterial under central generation;
distributed generation remains open. Results assume fixed capacities, a fixed heating curve, and radial networks; joint dispatch and
sizing are deferred to a companion study.
